course: dictionary
lesson: 3618
title: Filter: one word, many jobs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A filter is something with tiny holes that lets some things through and catches others. A word can have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one to use. «Filtering separates an undissolved solid from a liquid: the liquid passes through the tiny holes in filter paper, and the solid stays on it.» Here filter means a paper or cloth that catches the solid bits.
explain: Look at the words around filter. «Small round bowls are bad for goldfish, so they need a big tank with a filter.» The tank is full of water and the goldfish's waste makes the water dirty, so this filter must clean the water. The clues tell you what it does.
explain: Filter can also be a setting. «Phone filters find your face in real time and stick on dog ears.» No one is putting this through holes. It is a setting that changes how a picture looks. Ask yourself: is this a thing with holes, or a way of changing something? That question helps you pick the right meaning.
explain: Filter is also a verb, an action word. «A flamingo feeds with its head upside down, filtering tiny food through comb-like edges of its bill.» Here filtering means straining food out of the water. Try the meaning in the sentence: does straining food out of the water still make sense? Yes, so you have got it.
fact: A filter is a material or device with tiny holes that lets liquid or air pass but catches solid bits.
fact: To filter is to pass something through a filter to clean or separate it.
fact: A filter can also be a setting, like a photo filter or an internet filter, that changes or blocks something.
fact: Filter paper, water filters and air filters are all used to clean things.
fact: Filter out and filter in can mean to move slowly in small amounts, like sunlight filtered through leaves.
exercise: «Filtering separates an undissolved solid from a liquid: the liquid passes through the tiny holes in filter paper.» What happens to the liquid? | It turns into a solid | It disappears completely | *It passes through the tiny holes | It stays on the paper with the solid
exercise: «A flamingo feeds with its head upside down in the water, filtering tiny food through comb-like edges of its bill.» What is the flamingo doing with the food? | *Straining it out of the water | Throwing it away | Hiding it under a rock | Cooking it in the sun
exercise: «Small round bowls are bad for goldfish, so they need a big tank with a filter.» What is the filter for? | Lighting up the tank | Keeping the room warm | *Cleaning the water in the tank | Making the fish sing
test: Which one is a setting, not a thing with holes? | Air filter in a car | Coffee filter | Filter paper | *An internet filter that blocks websites
test: To filter a liquid means to | *Pass it through something with tiny holes to clean it | Freeze it solid | Heat it until it boils | Mix it with sand
exam: In «Filtering separates an undissolved solid from a liquid», what does separates mean? | Dries out | *Divides into different parts | Melts away | Mixes together
